<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Almodóvar del Río</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Almodóvar del Río is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalquivir">Guadalquivir</a> in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, dominated by a castle of Muslim origin on a hill above the river; its name comes from the Arabic al-Mudawwar, 'the round one'. The castle changed hands in the wars of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> period, and in 1226 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> pretender al-Bayyasi was killed nearby. The town passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> in the 13th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/almodovar-del-rio.jpg" alt="Almodóvar del Río" width="251" height="312" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Américo Toledano · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Almodovar-del-Rio-mapa.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Almodóvar del Río is a municipality in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, in the Valle Medio del <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalquivir">Guadalquivir</a>, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-morena">Sierra Morena</a> to the north and open countryside to the south. The town stands 121 metres above sea level and the municipality covers about 173 square kilometres. A large part of its northern area is protected as part of the Sierra de Hornachuelos Natural Park.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The oldest settlements date to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-paleolithic">Lower Paleolithic</a>, and a cave on Cerro del Castillo held remains from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcolithic">Chalcolithic</a>. Iberian pottery shows continued settlement, and a stone frieze of 4th–3rd century BCE shows a deer hunted by horsemen, followed by a cart pulled by donkeys. In Roman times the main settlement lay around Cerro del Castillo and is generally, with some problems, identified with Carbula, a town named by Pliny, which was integrated into the territory of Colonia Patricia Corduba. The area was marked by olive cultivation and an extensive trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a> exported in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amphora">amphorae</a>, and by underground <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aqueduct">aqueducts</a> at Cortijo Nuevo and Fuenreal. Settlement continued in the Visigothic period.</p><p>After the Muslim conquest a fortress was established on the hill in 740 and the town was called al-Mudawwar al-Adna, the round one, from the shape of the hill. In the 11th century it belonged first to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carmona">Carmona</a> and then to that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>; under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> it returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> and in the 12th and part of the 13th century it depended on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> court of Seville. In 1226 the Muslim king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baeza">Baeza</a> was decapitated at Almodóvar del Río, accused by the Almohads of treason for his alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-iii-of-castile">Ferdinand III of Castile</a> and León. In 1240 the town was incorporated into the Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> by treaty, and in 1243 it joined the Council of Córdoba.</p><p>In 1394 Frederick of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, an illegitimate son of Henry II, died as a prisoner in the castle. In the 15th century the castle belonged to the House of Baena y Cabra and had been a residence of Peter I and Henry II, and a prison. The name Almodóvar del Río first appears around 1473.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The municipality had 8,093 inhabitants in 2022. Its castle remains the dominant feature on the hill above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalquivir">Guadalquivir</a>. In the 19th century the end of the manorial regime and the confiscations of church and municipal land strengthened the large estates, which led to social conflict that appeared during the Second Republic and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Almodóvar del Río” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1226 al-Bayyasi was murdered by his own followers near <b>Almodóvar</b> while fleeing Córdoba.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-baeza">Taifa of Baeza</a></li><li><blockquote>Its rulers at times also held Écija and <b>Almodóvar</b> and allied with the Hammudids of Málaga against Seville.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-carmona">Taifa of Carmona</a></li></ul></section>
